  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nAt 1 PM CST, skies are becoming partly sunny across \nportions of central/north central South Dakota, but remain \ncloudy/overcast across northeast South Dakota into west central \nMinnesota. Winds are out of the north-northeast 5 to 15 mph with \nsome occasional higher gusts and temperatures where it's cloudy are \nmainly in the 30s to low 40s. The very far southwestern corner of \nthe CWA is sunny, and temperatures there are in the mid 40s to low \n50s this afternoon.\n\nThe forecast is dry throughout the entire 7-day forecast, thanks to \nupper level ridging dominating the steering flow winds aloft for the \nmajority of the forecast period. Surface high pressure over the \nregion means winds go light and variable tonight, resulting in some \nfog potential, if the low clouds go away allowing for maximum \nradiation cooling. Short term ensemble guidance is highlighting \ncentral and north central South Dakota with the best chance for fog \ntonight into Friday morning (~50-70% chance for visby less than 1 \nmile).\n\nTemperatures will be near normal on Friday. But the low level \nthermal advection pattern is still forecast to transition to a WAA \npattern Friday afternoon/evening which persists through the weekend. \nWarmer (above normal) temperatures then look to hold through the \nmiddle of next week, despite one or more frontal passages that \ntemporarily swing winds around to the west-northwest.\n\nCurrently, one such frontal passage appears to align with \nSaturday/night. Post frontal winds Saturday into Saturday night \ncould become blustery at times, particularly across the northern or \nnortheastern half of the CWA, with speeds potentially increasing to \n15 to 30 mph with gusts up to 40 mph not out of the question. Beyond \nSaturday/night, wind speeds appear to behave themselves throughout \nmost of the 7-day forecast. On the fire weather forecast side of \nthings, afternoon relative humidity values on Saturday may dip down \nto a range from ~40 percent to 50 percent when those stronger winds \nare happening. Current Grassland Fire Danger Index values top out in \nthe moderate to high range for Saturday. Monday could also see some \nlower afternoon relative humidity values. Otherwise, the rest of the \n7-day forecast appears to hold higher afternoon relative humidity \nvalues.",
